<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luenha River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ruenya River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Luenha is a river of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> that rises in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, where it is known as the Ruenya, and flows north-east into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a> above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tete">Tete</a>. Its confluence with the Zambezi was the site of Massangano, the fortified stronghold of the Afro-Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/da-cruz-family">Da Cruz family</a>, which controlled river traffic on the lower Zambezi in the nineteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luenha-river.jpg" alt="Luenha River" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hans Braxmeier &amp;amp; Peter in s, Location map:NordNordWest · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luenha_Mazowe_OSM.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
